---
import Layout from "../../layouts/BlogPost.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Steam, End to End"
  description="How heat travels through a plant — from boiler to point of use, and back again"
  pubDate={new Date("2026-04-17")}
  noindex={true}
>
  <p>
    Most people have never thought about where the heat in their hotel shower
    comes from. Or how a textile factory dries fabric at scale. Or why a
    refinery needs a dedicated team just to manage something invisible.
  </p>

  <p>
    The answer, in almost every case, is <strong>steam</strong> — pressurized water
    vapor, flowing through a network of pipes, doing the work that fire used to do,
    but safer, more precise, and at volumes fire can't match.
  </p>

  <p>This is the map of that system.</p>

  <h2>The ecosystem</h2>

  <p>
    A steam system isn't one machine. It's an ecosystem — multiple vendors,
    multiple disciplines, multiple decades of engineering, all converging on a
    single question: <em
      >how do we move heat reliably from one place to another?</em
    >
  </p>

  <pre
    class="mermaid">
{`flowchart TB
    subgraph Sources["Energy Source"]
      fuel["Fuel<br/>(gas · coal · biomass)"]
    end

    subgraph Generation["Steam Generation"]
      wt["Water Treatment<br/>(softener · deaerator)"]
      boiler["Boiler<br/>(firebox + water tubes)"]
    end

    subgraph Distribution["Distribution Network"]
      main["Main steam line<br/>(high pressure)"]
      prv["Pressure Reducing Valves<br/>(step down to use pressure)"]
      sep["Separator<br/>(dry steam only)"]
    end

    subgraph PoU["Point of Use"]
      kitchen["Kitchen<br/>(steam-jacketed kettle · combi oven)"]
      laundry["Laundry<br/>(washer · press · dryer)"]
      hvac["HVAC / Hot water<br/>(heat exchanger)"]
      process["Process heat<br/>(reactors · dryers · sterilizers)"]
    end

    subgraph Return["Recycle Loop"]
      traps["Steam Traps<br/>(drain condensate, hold steam)"]
      condensate["Condensate tank<br/>(60-90% water recovered)"]
    end

    fuel --> boiler
    wt --> boiler
    boiler --> main
    main --> sep --> prv
    prv --> kitchen
    prv --> laundry
    prv --> hvac
    prv --> process
    kitchen --> traps
    laundry --> traps
    hvac --> traps
    process --> traps
    traps --> condensate
    condensate --> wt

    classDef src fill:#2a2520,stroke:#d4a574,color:#e8e0d4
    classDef gen fill:#1c1a18,stroke:#d4a574,color:#e8e0d4
    classDef dist fill:#1c1a18,stroke:#8a8279,color:#e8e0d4
    classDef use fill:#1c1a18,stroke:#8a8279,color:#e8e0d4
    classDef ret fill:#1c1a18,stroke:#d4a574,color:#e8e0d4
    class fuel src
    class wt,boiler gen
    class main,prv,sep dist
    class kitchen,laundry,hvac,process use
    class traps,condensate ret`}
  </pre>

  <p>
    <em
      >Water comes in, gets cleaned, boils, flows out as steam, does work,
      condenses back to water, and returns to the beginning. The loop runs 24/7
      as long as the plant is operating.</em
    >
  </p>

  <h2>How steam is actually made</h2>

  <p>
    You burn something. Gas, coal, biomass, sometimes waste heat from another
    process. The fire heats tubes of water. The water boils. The steam collects
    at the top of a drum and leaves through a pipe. That's the whole story.
  </p>

  <p>
    But the water you feed in isn't the water from your tap. Raw water has
    minerals — calcium, magnesium — that form scale inside the boiler, reducing
    efficiency and eventually cracking pipes. It has dissolved oxygen that
    corrodes metal. So every boiler has a <strong>water treatment stage</strong
    >: softener to remove minerals, deaerator to strip oxygen, chemical dosing
    to prevent corrosion. Plants that skimp on water treatment spend twice as
    much on boiler repairs.
  </p>

  <h2>How steam is moved around</h2>

  <p>
    Steam leaves the boiler at high pressure — maybe 10 bar, around 184°C.
    That's too hot for most uses. You don't want your kitchen steamer running at
    184°C; you want it at maybe 144°C (3 bar) for consistent cooking.
  </p>

  <p>
    So at each point of use, a <strong>Pressure Reducing Valve</strong> drops the
    pressure to the exact level the equipment needs. The physics works in your favor
    here: steam has a fixed relationship between pressure and temperature, so controlling
    one controls the other.
  </p>

  <pre
    class="mermaid">
{`flowchart LR
    b["Boiler<br/>10 bar / 184°C"] --> m["Main header"]
    m --> p1["PRV 5 bar"] --> l["Laundry<br/>158°C"]
    m --> p2["PRV 3 bar"] --> k["Kitchen<br/>144°C"]
    m --> p3["PRV 2 bar"] --> h["Hot water<br/>133°C"]

    classDef node fill:#1c1a18,stroke:#d4a574,color:#e8e0d4
    class b,m,p1,p2,p3,l,k,h node`}
  </pre>

  <h2>How steam does work (without getting the food wet)</h2>

  <p>
    You almost never touch steam directly. Instead, steam flows through a
    <strong>heat exchanger</strong> — think of it as a metal wall between the steam
    and whatever you're heating. The steam condenses on one side of the wall, giving
    up its latent heat. The heat passes through the metal. The thing on the other
    side warms up. No contact.
  </p>

  <p>
    A steam-jacketed kettle is the simplest version: it's a pot with a double
    wall. Steam flows through the outer wall. Food cooks on the inner wall. Heat
    is even, temperature is capped, nothing burns. A 500-portion soup comes out
    exactly the same as a 5-portion soup.
  </p>

  <h2>The recycle loop nobody notices</h2>

  <p>
    After steam gives up its heat, it becomes water again — <em>condensate</em>.
    This water is already treated, already hot, already clean. Throwing it away
    is throwing money away.
  </p>

  <p>
    So every piece of steam-using equipment has a <strong>steam trap</strong>
    attached to its outlet. The trap is a clever little valve: it opens when water
    reaches it, closes when steam reaches it. Condensate drains, steam stays. The
    drained condensate flows back to a tank, pumps return it to the boiler, and the
    cycle begins again. A well-designed plant recovers 60-90% of its water this way.
  </p>

  <p>
    When steam traps fail — and they do, constantly — they either fail closed
    (water backs up, flooding the equipment) or fail open (live steam blows
    straight through, wasting everything). Surveys suggest that in plants
    without active management, <strong
      >30-40% of steam traps are malfunctioning at any given moment</strong
    >. That's the gap specialists were built to close.
  </p>

  <h2>Who makes what</h2>

  <p>
    No single company builds a steam system end-to-end. A plant assembles one
    from a lineup of specialists:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Boiler manufacturers</strong> — Miura, Takuma, Mitsubishi, Babcock &amp;
      Wilcox, Thermax
    </li>
    <li><strong>Water treatment</strong> — Ondeo, Nalco, Veolia</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Steam distribution specialists</strong> — TLV, Spirax Sarco, Watson
      McDaniel, Armstrong
    </li>
    <li><strong>Burners</strong> — Weishaupt, Riello</li>
    <li><strong>Controls / PLC</strong> — Siemens, Schneider, Yokogawa</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Insulation &amp; piping</strong> — Rockwool, Kingspan, plus local contractors
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    An EPC (Engineering, Procurement, Construction) contractor sits above all of
    them, coordinating the project. The plant owner usually just signs contracts
    and waits.
  </p>

  <h2>The TLV niche, specifically</h2>

  <p>
    TLV was founded in Japan in 1950 with one product: a steam trap that lasted
    ten times longer than anything else on the market. They priced it at three
    times the competition and dared plants to do the math. Plants did the math.
    TLV grew.
  </p>

  <p>
    Today they make valves, separators, software, and full condensate recovery
    systems — but the core bet is the same: <em
      >steam distribution is the part of the system that breaks most often, and
      the part operators understand least</em
    >. That's the wedge. Everything else is expansion.
  </p>

  <p>
    If boilers are the cloud provider — the big, obvious infrastructure — TLV is
    the observability and optimization layer. A niche most people have never
    heard of, making specialized equipment for an industry that doesn't get
    written about, quietly collecting 76 years of expertise that plants can't
    build in-house.
  </p>

  <p>
    <em
      >Steam is invisible. The people who manage it professionally are too.
      That's usually how it goes with infrastructure — the deeper it is, the
      fewer people know it exists.</em
    >
  </p>
</Layout>

